  "stdout": "-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 21905 Oct 14 23:40 /app/research/report.md\n424 /app/research/report.md\n# CRISPR-Mediated Ablation of MHC Class I and II Loci for the Generation of Universal \"Hypoimmunogenic\" Cell Lines for Allogeneic Therapy\n\n**Authors:** Research Proposal Draft  \n**Date:** 2025  \n**Domain:** Regenerative Medicine / Transplant Immunology / Gene Therapy\n\n---\n\n## Assumptions\n\n1. **CRISPR-Cas9 efficiency in progenitor cells:** We assume CRISPR-Cas9 editing can achieve ≥80% efficiency in human hematopoietic progenitor cells (HPCs) or iPSC-derived progenitors, with minimal off-target effects verified by whole-genome sequencing.\n\n2. **B2M/CIITA knockout ablation:** Complete loss of MHC Class I (via B2M KO) and MHC Class II (via CIITA KO) is achievable without triggering lethal cellular stress or compromising progenitor viability beyond acceptable thresholds.\n\n3. **CD47 tolerability:** Ectopic CD47 overexpression will not induce autoimmunity or thrombotic events at therapeutically relevant expression levels in vivo.\n\n4. **NK cell \"missing self\" recognition:** The absence of MHC Class I will indeed trigger NK cell activation via missing self-recognition, which can be sufficiently neutralized by concurrent CD47 expression and HLA-E engineering.\n\n5. **Clinical translation feasibility:** The edited cell line will retain multipotency and differentiation capacity while maintaining genetic stability over extended periods (>1 year) of in vitro culture and in vivo engraftment.\n\n---\n\n## Title\n\n**CRISPR-Mediated Ablation of MHC Class I and II Loci for the Generation of Universal \"Hypoimmunogenic\" Cell Lines for Allogeneic Therapy: Simultaneous Knockout of B2M and CIITA with CD47 Overexpression to Create \"Stealth\" Progenitor Cells Invisible to Adaptive and Innate Immunity**\n\n---\n\n## Background & Motivation\n\n### The Organ Transplantation Crisis\n\nThe shortage of compatible organs for transplant is a global crisis. In the United States alone, over 100,000 patients wait for organ transplants annually, with approximately 17 people dying each day while awaiting a match. Current therapeutic strategies for organ failure remain constrained by:\n\n- **Limited donor organ availability** — approximately 40-50% of transplanted organs remain unused due to compatibility issues or cold ischemia damage.\n- **Immunosuppression dependency** — recipients require lifelong immunosuppressive drugs, resulting in increased susceptibility to infections, malignancies, and metabolic complications.\n- **Rejection** — both hyperacute, acute, and chronic rejection limit transplant longevity and efficacy.\n\n### The Promise of Allogeneic Cell Therapy\n\n\"Off-the-shelf\" allogeneic cell therapies (e.g., CAR-T, stem cell-based regenerative treatments) represent a paradigm shift toward standardized, readily available therapies. However, their clinical utility is limited by:\n\n- **Graft-versus-Host Disease (GvHD)** — donor immune cells attack host tissues.\n- **Host-versus-Graft Rejection** — recipient immune systems recognize and eliminate transplanted cells.\n- **Individualized manufacturing** — autologous therapies require patient-specific production, increasing cost and timeline.\n\n### The \"Universal Donor\" Hypothesis\n\nA cell line engineered to be completely invisible to all human immune systems — both adaptive (T-cells) and innate (NK cells, macrophages) — would revolutionize regenerative medicine by enabling truly allogeneic therapies without immunosuppression.\n\n",
